    56	
    57	**Field rules:**
    58	- `file_hash`: SHA-256 hex of the entire `.mlck` file content.
    59	- `checkpoints` array: sorted by `creation_timestamp` ascending.
    60	- `log_shards` array: sorted by `file` name lexicographically.
    61	- `configs` array: sorted by `file` name lexicographically.
    62	- `step_range`: `[min_step, max_step]` across all entries in the shard.
    63	- `epoch_range`: `[min_epoch, max_epoch]` across all entries.
    64	- `time_range`: `[earliest_ts, latest_ts]` as ISO 8601 strings.
    65	
    66	---
    67	
    68	## 2. Timeline Output
    69	
    70	```json
    71	{
    72	  "status": "ok",
    73	  "total_entries": 1200,
    74	  "total_epochs": 5,
    75	  "step_range": [0, 50000],
    76	  "time_range": ["2024-01-15T08:00:00Z", "2024-01-17T14:00:00Z"],
    77	  "conflicts_resolved": 3,
    78	  "gaps": [
    79	    {
    80	      "after_step": 5000,
    81	      "before_step": 5200,
    82	      "epoch": 1,
    83	      "gap_steps": 200,
    84	      "after_ts": "2024-01-15T10:00:00Z",
    85	      "before_ts": "2024-01-15T13:00:00Z"
    86	    }
    87	  ],
    88	  "entries": [
    89	    {
    90	      "ts": "2024-01-15T08:00:00Z",
    91	      "epoch": 0,
    92	      "step": 0,
    93	      "loss": 6.9078,
    94	      "lr": 0.001,
    95	      "grad_norm": 12.34,
    96	      "acc": 0.001,
    97	      "event": "step",
    98	      "sources": ["shard_001.jsonl"],
    99	      "conflict": false
   100	    }
   101	  ]
   102	}
   103	```
   104	
   105	**Field rules:**
   106	- `entries`: sorted by `(epoch ASC, step ASC)`.
   107	- `sources`: list of shard filenames (basenames) that contained this `(epoch, step)`.
   108	- `conflict`: `true` if this entry was chosen by conflict resolution.
   109	- `gaps`: only gaps where `gap_steps > 100` within the same epoch.
   110	- Entries with missing optional fields omit them from the JSON (do not include null).
   111	
   112	---
   113	
   114	## 3. Verify Output
   115	
   116	```json
   117	{
   118	  "status": "ok",
   119	  "checkpoints": [
   120	    {
   121	      "file": "model_epoch1.mlck",
   122	      "valid": true,
   123	      "header_checksum_valid": true,
   124	      "tensor_checksums_valid": true,
   125	      "signature_valid": null,
   126	      "provenance_valid": true,
   127	      "issues": []
   128	    },
   129	    {
   130	      "file": "model_epoch3.mlck",
   131	      "valid": false,
   132	      "header_checksum_valid": true,
   133	      "tensor_checksums_valid": false,
   134	      "signature_valid": true,
   135	      "provenance_valid": true,
   136	      "issues": [
   137	        {"type": "tensor_checksum_mismatch", "detail": "encoder.layer2.weight"}
   138	      ]
   139	    }
   140	  ],
   141	  "summary": {
   142	    "total": 3,
   143	    "valid": 2,
   144	    "invalid": 1
   145	  }
   146	}
   147	```
   148	
   149	**Field rules:**
   150	- `checkpoints`: sorted by `file` name lexicographically.
   151	- `signature_valid`: `null` if the checkpoint is unsigned, `true`/`false` if signed.
   152	- `issues`: list of issue objects with `type` (string) and `detail` (string). For example, a tensor checksum mismatch appears as `{"type": "tensor_checksum_mismatch", "detail": "<tensor_name>"}`.
   153	- `valid`: `true` only if all applicable checks pass.
   154	
   155	---
   156	
   157	## 4. Detect Output
   158	
   159	```json
   160	{
   161	  "status": "ok",
   162	  "anomalies": [
   163	    {
   164	      "id": "ANOM-001",
   165	      "type": "gradient_spike",
   166	      "severity": "high",
   167	      "epoch": 2,
   168	      "step": 3456,
   169	      "details": {
   170	        "observed": 15.3,
   171	        "ewma_mean": 1.2,
   172	        "ewma_stddev": 0.3,
   173	        "z_score": 47.0
   174	      },
   175	      "evidence": {
   176	        "window_values": [1.1, 1.3, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4]
   177	      },
   178	      "source": "shard_002.jsonl"
   179	    },
   180	    {
   181	      "id": "ANOM-002",
   182	      "type": "tensor_checksum_mismatch",
   183	      "severity": "critical",
   184	      "details": {
   185	        "file": "model_epoch3.mlck",
   186	        "tensor_name": "encoder.layer2.weight",
   187	        "expected_checksum": "abcd1234...",
   188	        "actual_checksum": "efgh5678..."
   189	      },
   190	      "evidence": {},
   191	      "source": "model_epoch3.mlck",
   192	      "checkpoint": "model_epoch3.mlck"
   193	    }
   194	  ],
   195	  "summary": {
   196	    "total": 5,
   197	    "by_severity": {
   198	      "critical": 1,
   199	      "high": 2,
   200	      "medium": 1,
   201	      "info": 1
   202	    },
   203	    "by_type": {
   204	      "gradient_spike": 1,
   205	      "loss_divergence": 1,
   206	      "tensor_checksum_mismatch": 1,
   207	      "training_gap": 1,
   208	      "lr_anomaly": 1
   209	    }
   210	  }
   211	}
   212	```
   213	
   214	**Field rules:**
   215	- `id`: Sequential ID in format `ANOM-NNN`, assigned in detection order.
   216	- `anomalies`: sorted by severity (`critical` > `high` > `medium` > `low` > `info`), then `type` alphabetically, then `epoch` ascending, then `step` ascending.
   217	- Severity order is `critical` > `high` > `medium` > `low` > `info`. `--filter-severity LEVEL` returns anomalies at the specified severity level or above: `critical` returns only `critical`; `high` returns `critical` and `high`; `medium` returns `critical`, `high`, and `medium`; `low` returns `critical`, `high`, `medium`, and `low`; `info` returns all severities.
   218	- `type` values: `gradient_spike`, `loss_divergence`, `lr_anomaly`, `metric_regression`, `tensor_checksum_mismatch`, `header_checksum_mismatch`, `signature_invalid`, `provenance_chain_break`, `training_gap`, `dataset_hash_inconsistency`.
   219	- `details`: type-specific fields as defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`.
   220	- `evidence`: sibling of `id`, `type`, `severity`, `details`, and `source`; never nested inside `details`. Timeline-based anomalies include `window_values`, an array of up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of anomaly detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies, such as `tensor_checksum_mismatch`, must have an empty evidence object: `{}`.
[?2004hroot@n30009:/app# nl -ba docs/spec.md | sed -n '1,220p'
[?2004l     1	# mlforge - ML Model Artifact Forensics Engine
     2	
     3	## Overview
     4	
     5	`mlforge` is a command-line tool for forensic analysis of machine learning model artifacts. Given a directory of evidence (model checkpoints, training log shards, configuration files), mlforge reconstructs training timelines, detects anomalies, verifies artifact integrity, builds model lineage graphs, and produces evidence-backed forensic reports.
     6	
     7	## CLI Interface
     8	
     9	The binary is built as `mlforge` and placed at `/app/mlforge` (or on `$PATH` after `go install`).
    10	
    11	```
    12	mlforge <command> <evidence-dir> [flags]
    13	```
    14	
    15	### Commands
    16	
    17	| Command | Description |
    18	|---------|-------------|
    19	| `ingest` | Scan evidence directory, index all artifacts, print summary to stdout |
    20	| `timeline` | Reconstruct unified training timeline from log shards |
    21	| `verify` | Verify integrity of all checkpoint files |
    22	| `detect` | Run anomaly detection across all evidence |
    23	| `lineage` | Build and output model lineage DAG |
    24	| `query` | Query the unified timeline with filter expressions |
    25	| `report` | Generate full forensic report combining all analyses |
    26	
    27	### Global Flags
    28	
    29	| Flag | Type | Default | Description |
    30	|------|------|---------|-------------|
    31	| `--format` | string | `json` | Output format: `json`, `text`, or `csv` (`csv` is supported only by `detect`) |
    32	| `--verbose` | bool | false | Enable verbose diagnostic output to stderr |
    33	
    34	### Command-Specific Behavior
    35	
    36	#### `ingest`
    37	
    38	Scan `<evidence-dir>` recursively for:
    39	- `.mlck` files (model checkpoints)
    40	- `.jsonl` files (training log shards)
    41	- `.json` files matching `config_*.json` (training configurations)
    42	
    43	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Ingest Output".
    44	
    45	If the evidence directory does not exist or contains no recognizable artifacts, exit with code 1 and print an error to stderr.
    46	
    47	#### `timeline`
    48	
    49	Parse all `.jsonl` log shards in `<evidence-dir>`, merge entries into a unified timeline.
    50	
    51	**Merge Rules** (detailed in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md` section "Timeline Reconstruction"):
    52	- Entries are identified by `(epoch, step)` tuple
    53	- When multiple shards contain the same `(epoch, step)`, apply conflict resolution
    54	- Gaps in the step sequence are detected and reported
    55	
    56	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Timeline Output".
    57	
    58	#### `verify`
    59	
    60	For each `.mlck` file in `<evidence-dir>`:
    61	1. Parse the file according to the MLCK format (see `/app/docs/formats.md`)
    62	2. Validate the header checksum
    63	3. Validate each tensor's SHA-256 data checksum
    64	4. If the file is signed (Flags bit 0), validate the HMAC-SHA256 signature
    65	5. Validate provenance fields are well-formed
    66	
    67	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Verify Output".
    68	
    69	#### `detect`
    70	
    71	Run all anomaly detection rules (defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`) across the evidence:
    72	- Timeline-based anomalies (from log shards)
    73	- Checkpoint integrity anomalies (from .mlck files)
    74	- Cross-artifact anomalies (correlating checkpoints with logs)
    75	
    76	Every anomaly object includes a top-level `evidence` field. Timeline-based anomalies include `window_values` with up to 5 preceding metric values at the point of detection. `gradient_spike` uses preceding `grad_norm` values, `loss_divergence` uses preceding `loss` values, and other timeline-based anomalies may use an empty array when no meaningful preceding window is available. Checkpoint-based anomalies have an empty `evidence` object.
    77	
    78	Detect-specific flags:
    79	- `--format csv`: output CSV instead of JSON. The CSV header is exactly `id,type,severity,epoch,step,source,checkpoint`; one data row is emitted per anomaly. Leave `epoch`, `step`, or `checkpoint` empty when the anomaly does not have that field.
    80	- `--filter-severity LEVEL`: filter anomalies to show only those at the specified severity level or above. Severity order is `critical > high > medium > low > info`. For example, `--filter-severity high` shows only `critical` and `high` anomalies, while `--filter-severity critical` shows only `critical` anomalies. The returned JSON summary reflects the filtered anomaly list.
    81	
    82	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Detect Output".
    83	
    84	#### `lineage`
    85	
    86	Build a directed acyclic graph of model checkpoint relationships using the `parent_hash` field from each checkpoint's provenance section.
    87	
    88	**Rules**:
    89	- Each checkpoint is a node identified by its SHA-256 file hash
    90	- An edge `A -> B` exists if B's `parent_hash` matches A's file hash
    91	- Root nodes have empty `parent_hash`
    92	- Orphan nodes reference a parent not present in the evidence set
    93	- If a cycle is detected, report it as an anomaly (the graph should be a DAG)
    94	
    95	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Lineage Output".
    96	
    97	#### `query`
    98	
    99	```
   100	mlforge query <evidence-dir> "<expression>"
   101	```
   102	
   103	Filter the unified timeline using the query expression language defined in `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md` section "Query Language".
   104	
   105	Output (JSON to stdout): list of matching timeline entries.
   106	
   107	#### `diff`
   108	
   109	```
   110	mlforge diff <evidence-dir> <file1> <file2>
   111	```
   112	
   113	Compare two checkpoint files inside `<evidence-dir>` tensor by tensor and write JSON to stdout. `file1` and `file2` are checkpoint filenames relative to `<evidence-dir>`.
   114	
   115	For every tensor name present in either file, include one entry in `tensor_diffs`, sorted by tensor name lexicographically. `max_abs_diff` and `mean_abs_diff` are included only when both files contain the tensor and their shapes match.
   116	
   117	If either checkpoint filename does not exist under `<evidence-dir>`, exit non-zero and print an error to stderr.
   118	
   119	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Diff Output".
   120	
   121	#### `report`
   122	
   123	Generate a comprehensive forensic report by running all analyses (ingest, timeline, verify, detect, lineage) and combining results.
   124	
   125	Output (JSON to stdout): see `/app/docs/output_schema.md` section "Report Output".
   126	
   127	## Exit Codes
   128	
   129	| Code | Meaning |
   130	|------|---------|
   131	| 0 | Success |
   132	| 1 | Input error (missing directory, no artifacts, invalid arguments) |
   133	| 2 | Parse error (corrupt or unreadable artifact) |
   134	
   135	When a parse error occurs for individual artifacts within a batch operation (e.g., one corrupt checkpoint among several), the tool should continue processing remaining artifacts, report the error in the output, and exit with code 0. Exit code 2 is only for cases where the error prevents any meaningful output.
   136	
   137	## Build
   138	
   139	```bash
   140	cd /app && go build -o mlforge ./cmd/mlforge
   141	```
   142	
   143	## Cross-References
   144	
   145	- Binary formats: `/app/docs/formats.md`
   146	- Detection algorithms and query language: `/app/docs/analysis_rules.md`
   147	- JSON output schemas: `/app/docs/output_schema.md`
